{"data":[{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.13146","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.13146","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.13146","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zachary","familyName":"Wolfgram","name":"Wolfgram, Zachary","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jeffrey G.","familyName":"Ethier","name":"Ethier, Jeffrey G.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Matthew","familyName":"Grasinger","name":"Grasinger, Matthew","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Multi-scale Modeling of the Electro-viscoelasticity of Charged Polymers in Combined Flow and Electric Fields"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Soft Condensed Matter (cond-mat.soft)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Materials Science (cond-mat.mtrl-sci)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Statistical Mechanics (cond-mat.stat-mech)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T15:02:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:51:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-20T04:49:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-21T02:59:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-21T05:34:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-04-22T00:34:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-10-19T20:24:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-10-21T00:14:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1122/8.0001241","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The behavior of polymers in combined flow and electric fields underlies many manufacturing processes but remains poorly understood. We model charged polymers across scales, extending the Rouse model for a bead-spring chain to include a charge density distributed along the polymer chain, and derive the viscoelastic stress under homogeneous shear and electric fields. The viscosity increase depends on field-flow orientation, scaling quadratically with select components of the electric field strength, modulated by the effective charge sequence relaxation time and dielectric constant. Inspired by this, a new continuum model--the upper-convected electro-Maxwell (UCEM) model--is proposed. It resembles an upper-convected Maxwell model with polarization stresses expressed through an electric field dyadic subject to upper-convected time derivatives. The UCEM model captures the electro-rheological coupling for unentangled, salt-free melts at low shear rates and remains predictive into the moderately nonlinear regime ($Wi \\approx 2-4$) provided its parameters are treated as state-dependent material functions. We analyze the constitutive response for various flows under applied electric fields, discussing model limitations and demonstrating compliance with the second law of thermodynamics. Coarse-grained molecular dynamics (MD) simulations of Kremer-Grest chains confirm distinct relaxation timescales for chain dynamics versus charge redistribution, consistent with UCEM predictions. Critically, the upper-convected time derivative of the electric field dyadic is required in the stress evolution to account for stretching and rotation of charge pairs in flow. This reproduces the viscosity scaling observed in both the Rouse and MD results, whereas standard continuum formulations without these terms fail to capture this scaling."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"20 pages, 8 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.13146","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-09-17T01:56:58Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:56:59Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-21T03:13:31Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12746","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12746","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12746","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tony","familyName":"Lindeberg","name":"Lindeberg, Tony","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zahra","familyName":"Babaiee","name":"Babaiee, Zahra","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peyman M.","familyName":"Kiasari","name":"Kiasari, Peyman M.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the \"master key filters hypothesis\" for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T07:04:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:28:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-23T14:44:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-21T01:10:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1007/s10851-026-01290-0","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This paper presents the results of analysing and modelling a set of 8 ``master key filters'', which have been extracted by applying a clustering approach to the receptive fields learned in depthwise-separable deep networks based on the ConvNeXt architecture.\n For this purpose, we first compute spatial spread measures in terms of weighted mean values and weighted variances of the absolute values of the learned filters, which support the working hypotheses that: (i) the learned filters can be modelled by separable filtering operations over the spatial domain, and that (ii) the spatial offsets of the those learned filters that are non-centered are rather close to half a grid unit. Then, we model the clustered ``master key filters'' in terms of difference operators applied to a spatial smoothing operation in terms of the discrete analogue of the Gaussian kernel, and demonstrate that the resulting idealized models of the receptive fields show good qualitative similarity to the learned filters.\n This modelling is performed in two different ways: (i) using possibly different values of the scale parameters in the coordinate directions for each filter, and (ii) using the same value of the scale parameter in both coordinate directions. Then, we perform the actual model fitting by either (i) requiring spatial spread measures in terms of spatial variances of the absolute values of the receptive fields to be equal, or (ii) minimizing the discrete $l_1$- or $l_2$-norms between the idealized receptive field models and the learned filters.\n Complementary experimental results then demonstrate the idealized models of receptive fields have good predictive properties for replacing the learned filters by idealized filters in depthwise-separable deep networks, thus showing that the learned filters in depthwise-separable deep networks can be well approximated by discrete scale-space filters."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"25 pages, 5 figures, 11 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12746","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-09-17T01:47:32Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:47:33Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-21T03:13:30Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.13222","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.13222","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.13222","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Claudio","familyName":"Landim","name":"Landim, Claudio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jungkyoung","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Jungkyoung","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mauro","familyName":"Mariani","name":"Mariani, Mauro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"The Gamma Expansion of the Level Two Large Deviation Rate Functional for Reversible Diffusion Processes"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Probability (math.PR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Statistical Mechanics (cond-mat.stat-mech)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"60F10, 60J45, 60J60","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T16:31:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:56:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T03:31:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-18T00:19:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-18T12:44:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-10-20T01:19:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Fix a smooth Morse function $U\\colon \\mathbb{R}^{d}\\to\\mathbb{R}$ with finitely many critical points, and consider the solution of the stochastic differential equation \\begin{equation*}\n d\\boldsymbol{x}_ε(t)=-\\nabla U(\\boldsymbol{x}_ε(t))\\,dt \\,+\\,\\sqrt{2ε}\\, d\\boldsymbol{w}_{t}\\,, \\end{equation*} where $(\\boldsymbol{w}_{t})_{t\\ge0}$ represents a $d$-dimensional Brownian motion, and $ε\u0026gt;0$ a small parameter. Denote by $\\mathcal{P}(\\mathbb{R}^{d})$ the space of probability measures on $\\mathbb{R}^d$, and by $\\mathcal{I}_ε \\colon \\mathcal{P}(\\mathbb{R}^{d})\\to[0,\\,\\infty]$ the Donsker--Varadhan level two large deviations rate functional. We express $\\mathcal{I}_ε$ as $\\mathcal{I}_ε= ε^{-1} \\mathcal{J}^{(-1)} + \\mathcal{J}^{(0)} + \\sum_{1\\le p\\le \\mathfrak{q}} (1/θ^{(p)}_ε) \\, \\mathcal{J}^{(p)}$, where $\\mathcal{J}^{(p)}\\colon \\mathcal{P}(\\mathbb{R}^d) \\to [0,+\\infty]$ stand for rate functionals independent of $ε$ and $θ^{(p)}_ε$ for sequences such that $θ^{(1)}_ε\\to\\infty$, $θ^{(p)}_ε/ θ^{(p+1)}_ε\\to 0$ for $1\\le p\u0026lt; \\mathfrak{q}$. The speeds $θ^{(p)}_ε$ correspond to the time-scales at which the diffusion $\\boldsymbol{x}_ε(\\cdot)$ exhibits a metastable behavior, while the functional $\\mathcal{J}^{(p)}$ represents the level two large deviations rate functional of the finite-state, continuous-time Markov chains which describe the evolution of the diffusion $\\boldsymbol{x}_ε(\\cdot)$ among the wells in the time-scale $θ^{(p)}_ε$."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"67 pages, 2 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.13222","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:58:44Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:58:45Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-20T05:15:09Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12586","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12586","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12586","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jian","familyName":"Xiao","name":"Xiao, Jian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ji","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Ji","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ming","familyName":"Zeng","name":"Zeng, Ming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongbo","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Hongbo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xingwang","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Xingwang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arumugam","familyName":"Nallanathan","name":"Nallanathan, Arumugam","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Channel Estimation for Rydberg Atomic Quantum Receivers: Unrolled Phase Retrieval from Holographic Snapshots"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Theory (cs.IT)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T02:23:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:16:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-20T03:37:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-21T01:37:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-11T12:41:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-10-20T01:58:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1109/lsp.2026.3685170","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"A model-driven deep learning framework is proposed for channel estimation in Rydberg atomic quantum receivers (RAQRs) based on the measurement of holographic snapshots. Specifically, we develop a Transformer-based unrolling architecture, termed URformer, to solve the non-linear biased phase retrieval problem, which is derived by unrolling a stabilized variant of the expectation-maximization Gerchberg-Saxton (EM-GS) algorithm. Each layer of the proposed URformer incorporates three trainable modules: 1) a learnable filter network that replaces the fixed Bessel kernel in the classic EM-GS algorithm; 2) a trainable gating mechanism that adaptively combines classic updates to ensure training stability; and 3) an efficient channel Transformer module that learns to correct residual errors by capturing non-local channel dependencies. Numerical results demonstrate that the proposed URformer significantly outperforms classic iterative algorithms and conventional black-box neural networks with less pilot overhead."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12586","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-09-17T01:43:50Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:43:51Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-20T05:15:08Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12426","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12426","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12426","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"B. M.","familyName":"Rodríguez-Lara","name":"Rodríguez-Lara, B. M.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"F. E.","familyName":"Becerra","name":"Becerra, F. E.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Mapping continuous-variable quantum states onto optical scalar beams"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Optics (physics.optics)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T20:28:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-10-20T00:23:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1088/2515-7647/ae2fa4","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Optical fields provide an accessible platform to explore connections between classical and quantum mechanics. We introduce a group-theoretic framework based on the $\\mathrm{su}(1,1)$ Lie algebra to construct classical analogs of continuous-variable quantum states using the spatial degree of freedom of paraxial scalar beams. Our framework maps squeezed number states onto scalar beams expanded in orthonormal Gaussian modal bases, encompassing both Gaussian and non-Gaussian classical analogs, including one- and two-mode squeezed beams. To characterize the structural changes induced by squeezing, we examine phase-space redistribution through Fourier analysis and optical Wigner distribution functions. We derive analytical expressions for the waist, curvature, and Gouy phase of two-mode squeezed Laguerre-Gaussian beams, and establish a relation between the number of accessible modes and the achievable squeezing under finite numerical aperture. While squeezing introduces spatial and spectral correlations that reshape the beam structure, these beams remain constrained by the diffraction limit, as confirmed by the numerical propagation of apodized beams. These correlations give rise to classical entanglement. We establish a classical analog of the Duan--Simon inseparability criterion for continuous-variable two-mode Gaussian states. For non-Gaussian squeezed states, we analyze the marginal optical Wigner distribution functions and identify phase-space features, such as negativity, that act as witnesses of classical continuous-variable entanglement. Our framework unifies classical analogs of continuous-variable quantum states through beam engineering, enabling quantum-inspired applications in optical imaging, metrology, and communication."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"32 pages, 11 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12426","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-09-17T01:40:09Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:40:10Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-20T05:15:07Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12717","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12717","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12717","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hans Michael","familyName":"Christensen","name":"Christensen, Hans Michael","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Johannes","familyName":"Agerskov","name":"Agerskov, Johannes","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Frederik","familyName":"Nathan","name":"Nathan, Frederik","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Ancilla-train quantum algorithm for simulating non-Markovian open quantum systems"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Quantum Physics (quant-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Statistical Mechanics (cond-mat.stat-mech)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T06:18:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:26:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-19T15:29:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-14T04:01:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1103/dc52-844x","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We present a quantum algorithm for simulating open quantum systems coupled to Gaussian environments valid for any configuration and coupling strength. The algorithm is applicable to problems with strongly coupled, or non-Markovian, environments, problems with multiple environments out of mutual equilibrium, and problems with time-dependent Hamiltonians. We show that the algorithm can reproduce the true dynamics of such problems at arbitrary accuracy and, for a broad range of problems, only adds a minor resource cost relative to Trotterized time evolution; the cost is low-degree polynomial in the inverse target accuracy. The algorithm is based on the insight that any Gaussian environment can be represented as a train of ancillary qubits that sequentially interact with the system through a time-local coupling, given by the convolution square root of the bath correlation function; this is a secondary result of our work. Our results open up new applications of quantum computers for efficient simulation of non-equilibrium and open quantum systems."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"10 pages, 18 pages in appendices. 4 figures and 1 table"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12717","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-09-17T01:46:52Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:46:53Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-14T08:19:35Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12651","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12651","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12651","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Stephan","familyName":"Wong","name":"Wong, Stephan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Simon","familyName":"Betzold","name":"Betzold, Simon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sven","familyName":"Hofling","name":"Hofling, Sven","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alexander","familyName":"Cerjan","name":"Cerjan, Alexander","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Dynamically reconfigurable topological routing in nonlinear photonic systems"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Optics (physics.optics)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Mesoscale and Nanoscale Physics (cond-mat.mes-hall)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T04:12:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-10-14T01:32:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1038/s41377-025-02108-1","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The propagation path of topologically protected states is bound to the interface between regions with different topology, and as such, the functionality of linear photonic devices leveraging these states is fixed during fabrication. Here, we propose a mechanism for dynamic control over a driven dissipative system's local topology, yielding reconfigurable topological interfaces and thus tunable paths for protected routing. We illustrate our approach in non-resonantly pumped polariton lattices, where the nonlinear interaction between the polaritons and the exciton reservoir due to non-resonant pumping can yield a dynamical change of the topology. Moreover, using a continuous model of the polariton system based on a driven-dissipative Gross-Pitaevskii equation alongside the spectral localizer framework, we show that the local changes in the nonlinear non-Hermitian system's topology are captured by a local Chern marker. Looking forward, we anticipate such reconfigurable topological routing will enable the realization of novel classes of topological photonic devices."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"10 pages, 4 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12651","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-09-17T01:45:21Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:45:22Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-14T08:19:34Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12630","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12630","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12630","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haozhi","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Haozhi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weiying","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Weiying","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hangyu","familyName":"Ye","name":"Ye, Hangyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daixun","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Daixun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jitao","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Jitao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yunsong","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yunsong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Leyuan","familyName":"Fang","name":"Fang, Leyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"High-Energy Concentration for Federated Learning in Frequency Domain"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T03:49:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:20:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:41:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-14T02:32:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1109/jiot.2026.3738828","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Federated Learning (FL) presents significant potential for collaborative optimization without data sharing. Since synthetic data is sent to the server, leveraging the popular concept of dataset distillation, this FL framework protects real data privacy while alleviating data heterogeneity. However, such methods are still challenged by the redundant information and noise in entire spatial-domain designs, which inevitably increases the communication burden. In this paper, we propose a novel Frequency-Domain aware FL method with high-energy concentration (FedFD) to address this problem. Our FedFD is inspired by the discovery that the discrete cosine transform predominantly distributes energy to specific regions, referred to as high-energy concentration. The principle behind FedFD is that low-energy like high-frequency components usually contain redundant information and noise, thus filtering them helps reduce communication costs and optimize performance. Our FedFD is mathematically formulated to preserve the low-frequency components using a binary mask, facilitating an optimal solution through frequency-domain distribution alignment. In particular, real data-driven synthetic classification is imposed into the loss to enhance the quality of the low-frequency components. On five image and speech datasets, FedFD achieves superior performance than state-of-the-art methods while reducing communication costs. For example, on the CIFAR-10 dataset with Dirichlet coefficient $α= 0.01$, FedFD achieves a minimum reduction of 37.78\\% in the communication cost, while attaining a 10.88\\% performance gain."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12630","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-09-17T01:44:52Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:44:53Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-14T08:19:33Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12709","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12709","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12709","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"He","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, He","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yueyan","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yueyan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xin","familyName":"Guan","name":"Guan, Xin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jie","familyName":"Cai","name":"Cai, Jie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"John M.","familyName":"Carroll","name":"Carroll, John M.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Harnessing the Power of AI in Qualitative Research: Role Assignment, Engagement, and User Perceptions of AI-Generated Follow-Up Questions in Semi-Structured Interviews"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T05:59:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:25:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-10-07T08:35:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-08T00:45:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Semi-structured interviews highly rely on the quality of follow-up questions, yet interviewers' knowledge and skills may limit their depth and potentially affect outcomes. While many studies have shown the usefulness of large language models (LLMs) for qualitative analysis, their possibility in the data collection process remains underexplored. We adopt an AI-driven \"Wizard-of-Oz\" setup to investigate how real-time LLM support in generating follow-up questions shapes semi-structured interviews. Through a study with 17 participants, we examine the value of LLM-generated follow-up questions, the evolving division of roles, relationships, collaborative behaviors, and responsibilities between interviewers and AI. Our findings (1) provide empirical evidence of the strengths and limitations of AI-generated follow-up questions (AGQs); (2) introduce a Human-AI collaboration framework in this interview context; and (3) propose human-centered design guidelines for AI-assisted interviewing. We position LLMs as complements, not replacements, to human judgment, and highlight pathways for integrating AI into qualitative data collection."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"This paper has been accepted to IJHCS"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12709","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:46:40Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:46:41Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-08T03:03:40Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12300","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12300","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12300","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Adnaan","familyName":"Nauthoo","name":"Nauthoo, Adnaan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peter","familyName":"Dunsby","name":"Dunsby, Peter","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Álvaro","familyName":"de la Cruz-Dombriz","name":"de la Cruz-Dombriz, Álvaro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Past and future isotropization in $f(R)$ dark energy models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"General Relativity and Quantum Cosmology (gr-qc)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T18:00:00Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:02:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-10-06T08:38:00Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-07T00:52:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1103/r2sq-44mh","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We present a dynamical system analysis for the homogeneous and anisotropic Bianchi I cosmology in the context of Hu-Sawicki $f(R)$ gravity with parameters $\\{n,C_{1}\\}=\\{1,1\\}$. Deriving the corresponding four-dimensional dynamical system from the field equations allows us to find the fixed points of the system in both the vacuum and matter cases alongside their stability. This approach also provides analytical expressions for both the cosmic expansion and shear of each fixed point. More importantly, it allows us to address one of the least satisfying aspects of the $Λ$CDM picture: the apparent fine-tuning of the initial state. We show that, for the chosen parameters, Hu-Sawicki gravity allows for isotropization both in the past and in the future for both vacuum and matter, demonstrating that Hu-Sawicki gravity may alleviate the anisotropy-related initial-condition problem for a subsequent inflationary phase. For the matter case, the analysis was subdivided into the dust and radiation cases, and we show that both cases exhibit past and future isotropization. The obtained results also revealed interesting aspects of the phase space such as the existence and phenomenology of lines and two-dimensional sheets of fixed points."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Version published in PRD, minor changes, conclusions unchanged"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12300","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-09-17T01:37:17Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:37:18Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-07T03:15:38Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.13141","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.13141","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.13141","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"László","familyName":"Csató","name":"Csató, László","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David","familyName":"Winkelmann","name":"Winkelmann, David","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Encouraging offensive play: A hidden benefit of incomplete round-robin tournaments"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"General Economics (econ.GN)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Physics and Society (physics.soc-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Applications (stat.AP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Economics and business","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"62P20, 90-10, 90B90, 91B14","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T14:56:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:51:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-10-05T07:18:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-06T02:01:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The Union of European Football Associations (UEFA) fundamentally reformed its club competitions from the 2024/25 season onward. The traditional group stage has been replaced by an incomplete round-robin league phase, in which all teams are ranked in a single table based on matches played against a subset of the other teams. Early evidence from the first two seasons of the UEFA Champions League in the new format indicates a marked decline in the proportion of drawn matches compared to the old format. Inspired by this observation, our paper examines how tournament design shapes the incentives to play offensively. We develop a simulation framework to compare the marginal gain from scoring a goal and loss from conceding a goal in terms of the probability of reaching critical ranking thresholds, using the initial scoreline of 0-0 as the reference point. We find robustly stronger incentives for offensive play in the new Champions League design. Roughly half of the increase is attributable to the modified thresholds, but the other half stems directly from replacing the group stage with an incomplete round-robin league. Our study is the first demonstration in the literature that the tournament format can materially affect team behaviour on the field."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"30 pages, 7 figures, 8 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.13141","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:56:51Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:56:52Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-06T05:41:52Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12613","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12613","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12613","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Abhishek","familyName":"Chakraborty","name":"Chakraborty, Abhishek","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Angelia","familyName":"Nedić","name":"Nedić, Angelia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Randomized Feasibility-Update Algorithms for Stochastic Variational Inequality Problems"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Optimization and Control (math.OC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T03:22:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:19:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-16T23:02:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-20T00:11:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-01T22:38:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-10-05T00:17:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This paper considers stochastic monotone variational inequalities whose feasible region is the intersection of a (possibly infinite) number of convex functional level sets. A projection-based approach or direct Lagrangian-based techniques for such problems can be computationally expensive if not impossible to implement. To deal with the problem, we consider randomized methods that avoid the projection step on the whole constraint set by employing random feasibility updates. In particular, we propose and analyze modified stochastic Korpelevich and Popov methods for solving monotone stochastic VIs. We introduce a modified dual gap function and prove the convergence rates with respect to this function. We illustrate the performance of the methods in simulations on a zero-sum matrix game."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12613","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:44:28Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:44:29Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-05T02:42:57Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12760","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12760","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12760","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Allen","familyName":"Schmaltz","name":"Schmaltz, Allen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Similarity-Distance-Magnitude Activations"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T07:19:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:29:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-30T06:08:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-31T00:27:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-03T19:51:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-12-05T01:04:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-04-16T17:12:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-04-17T01:04:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-06-08T05:40:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2026-06-09T01:23:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2026-09-30T01:41:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v6"},{"date":"2026-10-01T00:33:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v6"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.18653/v1/2026.findings-acl.1109","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"6","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We introduce the Similarity-Distance-Magnitude (SDM) activation function, a more robust and interpretable formulation of the standard softmax activation function, adding Similarity (i.e., correctly predicted depth-matches into training) awareness and Distance-to-training-distribution awareness to the existing output Magnitude (i.e., decision-boundary) awareness, and enabling interpretability-by-exemplar via dense matching. We further introduce the SDM estimator, based on a data-driven partitioning of the class-wise empirical CDFs via the SDM activation, to control the class- and prediction-conditional accuracy among selective classifications. When used as the final-layer activation over pre-trained language models for selective classification, the SDM estimator is more robust to covariate shifts and out-of-distribution inputs than existing calibration methods using softmax activations, while remaining informative over in-distribution data."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Published in Findings of the Association for Computational Linguistics: ACL 2026. 22 pages, 8 tables, 2 algorithms. (v6 adds Appendix A.10 and Alg. 2.) arXiv admin note: substantial text overlap with arXiv:2502.20167"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12760","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":5,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-09-17T01:47:52Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:47:52Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-01T03:46:39Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12654","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12654","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12654","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zi-Yang","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Zi-Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei E. I.","familyName":"Sha","name":"Sha, Wei E. I.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hang","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Hang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Anomalous inverse Faraday effect for graphene quantum dots in optical vortices"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Mesoscale and Nanoscale Physics (cond-mat.mes-hall)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T04:25:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-10-01T00:34:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1103/jzck-plc9","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Chiral photon interactions with two-dimensional (2D) materials enable unprecedented control of quantum phenomena. In this paper, we report anomalous inverse Faraday effects (IFE) in graphene quantum dots (GQDs) under linearly polarized optical vortex illumination, where transferred orbital angular momentum (OAM) generates light-induced magnetic moments. Employing our recently developed time-dependent quantum perturbation framework [Phys. Rev. B 110, 085425 (2024)], we demonstrate a counterintuitive observation: some reversed magnetic moments at off-axis positions occur-manifested as counter-rotating currents to the vortex helical wavefront. Phase-difference analysis and eigenmode decomposition resolve this anomaly, revealing that the OAM transfer efficiency is orders of magnitude weaker than its spin counterpart. This work establishes a new paradigm for optical OAM-to-magnetization conversion in quantum-engineered 2D systems."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"8 pages, 4 figure"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12654","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-09-17T01:45:25Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:45:26Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-01T03:46:38Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12672","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12672","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12672","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shaz","familyName":"Furniturewala","name":"Furniturewala, Shaz","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arkaitz","familyName":"Zubiaga","name":"Zubiaga, Arkaitz","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Towards Inclusive Toxic Content Moderation: Addressing Vulnerabilities to Adversarial Attacks in Toxicity Classifiers Tackling LLM-generated Content"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T04:51:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:23:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-23T09:33:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-26T00:30:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-27T23:01:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-09-29T01:57:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Adversarial perturbations can reduce state-of-the-art toxicity classifiers to near-zero accuracy, yet existing defences treat models as black boxes. We apply mechanistic interpretability to toxicity classification for the first time, identifying the internal attention-head circuits responsible for both correct classification and adversarial vulnerability. Across a 2$\\times$2 factorial study (BERT $\\times$ RoBERTa) $\\times$ (Jigsaw $\\times$ ToxiGen), extended to Llama Guard~2 (8B), we show that zeroing a single attention head recovers up to 70.4 pp of adversarial accuracy for RoBERTa on Jigsaw and 37.3 pp for Llama Guard 2 on ToxiGen, at $\\leq$0.6 pp clean cost. Vulnerable heads generalise to held-out examples within $\\leq$1 pp, and a class-imbalance sweep confirms they act as selective toxic-class detectors. Head suppression matches or outperforms adversarial training on Jigsaw; data augmentation dominates on ToxiGen: a dataset-specific reversal explained by whether the classifier encodes a concentrated bottleneck or a distributed circuit. Demographic analysis across 20 Jigsaw and 13 ToxiGen minority groups reveals structurally unequal adversarial vulnerability, exposing mechanistically traceable fairness gaps in current toxicity classifiers."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12672","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:45:49Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:45:50Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-29T06:56:16Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.13173","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.13173","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.13173","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["translator"],"givenName":"Leonhard","familyName":"Euler","name":"Euler, Leonhard","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["translator"],"givenName":"Jonathan David","familyName":"Evans","name":"Evans, Jonathan David","nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Euler's explorations of extremal ellipses"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"History and Overview (math.HO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Metric Geometry (math.MG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T15:24:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:53:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-09-22T15:04:25Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-29T02:38:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.56031/2693-9908.1109","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In the 1770s, Euler wrote a series of papers (E563, E691 and E692) about finding the ellipse with minimal area or perimeter in the family of all ellipses passing through a fixed set of points. This is a translation of all three papers from the original Latin, together with a commentary which discusses Euler's results and an appendix which addresses a question from E691 which Euler left for others to consider."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"63 pages; v2 accepted version"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.13173","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-09-17T01:57:37Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:57:37Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-29T06:56:16Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12357","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12357","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12357","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mikhail","familyName":"Lyubich","name":"Lyubich, Mikhail","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jacob","familyName":"Mazor","name":"Mazor, Jacob","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sabyasachi","familyName":"Mukherjee","name":"Mukherjee, Sabyasachi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Antiholomorphic correspondences and mating II: Shabat polynomial slices"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Dynamical Systems (math.DS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Complex Variables (math.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"37E25, 37F05, 37F10, 37F12, 37F20, 37F25, 37F31, 37F32, 37F46 (primary), 14H57, 30C10, 30C20, 30C50, 30C75, 30D05 (secondary)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T18:41:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T10:35:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-09-25T14:57:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-28T00:58:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We study natural one-parameter families of antiholomorphic correspondences arising from Shabat polynomials, indexed by rooted dessins d'enfants. The actions of our correspondences are governed by the dynamics of certain Schwarz reflection maps associated with univalent restrictions of the Shabat polynomials. We prove that the parameter spaces are topological quadrilaterals, giving a partial description of the univalency loci for the Shabat polynomials. We show that the escape loci of our parameter spaces admit natural real-analytic parametrizations by disks. We proceed with designing a puzzle structure (dual to the indexing dessin) for non-renormalizable maps, yielding combinatorial rigidity in these classes. Then we develop a renormalization theory for pinched (anti-)polynomial-like maps in order to describe all combinatorial Multibrot and Multicorn copies contained in our connectedness loci (a curious feature of these parameter spaces is the presence of multiple period-one copies). Finally, we construct locally connected combinatorial models for the connectedness loci into which the indexing dessins naturally embed."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Final accepted version, to appear in Ann. Fac. Sci. Toulouse Math. (6)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12357","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:38:35Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:38:36Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-28T02:32:25Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12737","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12737","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12737","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nikolai","familyName":"Piskunov","name":"Piskunov, Nikolai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Adam D.","familyName":"Rains","name":"Rains, Adam D.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Linn","familyName":"Boldt-Christmas","name":"Boldt-Christmas, Linn","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"TSD: An inverse problem approach for recovering the exoplanetary atmosphere transmission spectrum from high-resolution spectroscopy"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Earth and Planetary Astrophysics (astro-ph.EP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Instrumentation and Methods for Astrophysics (astro-ph.IM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T06:51:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:28:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-09-23T07:17:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-24T00:34:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.3847/1538-4357/aea6b2","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Our ability to observe, detect, and characterize exoplanetary atmospheres has grown by leaps and bounds over the last 20 years, aided largely by developments in astronomical instrumentation; improvements in data analysis techniques; and an increase in the sophistication and availability of spectroscopic models. Over this time, detections have been made for a number of important molecular species across a range of wavelengths and spectral resolutions. Ground-based observations at high resolution are particularly valuable due to the high contrast achievable between the stellar spectral continuum and the cores of resolved exoplanet absorption features. However, the model-independent retrieval of such features remains a major hurdle in data analysis, with traditional methods being limited by both the choice of algorithm used to remove the non-exoplanetary components of the signal, as well as the accuracy of model template spectra used for cross-correlation. Here we present a new algorithm TSD (Transmission Spectroscopy Decomposition) formulated as an inverse problem in order to minimize the number of assumptions and theoretically modelled components included in the retrieval. Instead of cross-correlation with pre-computed template exoplanet spectra, we rely on high spectral resolution and instrument stability to distinguish between the stellar, exoplanetary, and telluric components and velocity frames in the sequence of absorption spectra taken during multiple transits. We demonstrate the performance of our new method using both simulated and real K band observations from ESO's VLT/CRIRES+ instrument, and present results obtained from two transits of the highly-inflated super-Neptune WASP-107 b which orbits a nearby K7V star."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"37 pages, 11 figures. Accepted in ApJ"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12737","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-09-17T01:47:19Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:47:20Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-24T02:49:00Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12404","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12404","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12404","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"N.","familyName":"Lightfoot","name":"Lightfoot, N.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"A.","familyName":"Diaz-Torres","name":"Diaz-Torres, A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"P.","familyName":"Stevenson","name":"Stevenson, P.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Thermal effects on stellar neutron capture reactions: a quantum dynamical approach"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Nuclear Theory (nucl-th)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T19:50:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:06:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T07:16:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-18T00:27:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-02T11:58:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-01-05T01:39:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-03-25T19:26:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-03-27T00:07:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-05-21T21:01:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2026-05-25T00:08:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2026-07-31T10:05:00Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v6"},{"date":"2026-08-03T00:36:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v6"},{"date":"2026-09-21T18:09:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v7"},{"date":"2026-09-23T00:05:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v7"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"7","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The neutron capture process plays a vital role in creating the heavy elements in the universe. Astrophysical environments involved in these processes are characterized by two distinct reaction mechanisms: the slow and rapid neutron capture processes. In this work, the slow neutron capture process is described with the time-dependent coupled channels wave-packet (TDCCWP) method that uses both a many-body nuclear potential and an initial temperature-dependent state to account for the thermal environment. To evaluate the role of a mixed and entangled initial state in the temperature-dependent neutron capture cross section, TDCCWP calculations are compared with those from the coupled-channels density matrix (CCDM) method based on the Lindblad master equation. The importance of including temperature in the initial wave-function of the TDCCWP approach is compared to a thermalisation of the reaction rate using a Hauser-Feshbach style approach. TDCCWP calculations indicate a decrease of the n+$^{188}$Os capture cross section with increasing temperature, along with a decrease in reaction rates for the highest thermal energies studied, which are contrary to Hauser-Feshbach calculations and important in the rapid neutron capture process. The physical reason for this discrepancy is the key role of the dynamical nuclear coupling between the thermally populated states of the target nucleus, which is neglected in the Hauser-Feshbach approach, but creates a dominant neutron capture pathway with increased neutron speed and thus reduces the neutron capture cross section."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"13 pages, 12 figures, 3 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12404","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":6,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:39:39Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:39:40Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-23T02:46:57Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.13271","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.13271","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.13271","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yashika","familyName":"Ghai","name":"Ghai, Yashika","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"D.","familyName":"Del-Castillo-Negrete","name":"Del-Castillo-Negrete, D.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"D. A.","familyName":"Spong","name":"Spong, D. A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"M. T.","familyName":"Beidler","name":"Beidler, M. T.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Runaway electron interactions with whistler waves in tokamak plasmas: energy-dependent transport scaling"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Plasma Physics (physics.plasm-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T17:29:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:58:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-09-21T15:50:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-22T02:11:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Resonant interactions between high energy runaway electrons (REs) and whistler waves are a promising mechanism for RE mitigation in tokamak plasmas. While prior studies have largely relied on quasi-linear diffusion models in simplified geometries, we present a first-principles-informed framework that models RE-whistler interactions in a 3D tokamak equilibrium. This is achieved by coupling AORSA, which computes whistler eigenmodes for a given tokamak plasma equilibrium, and KORC, a kinetic orbit code that tracks full orbit RE trajectories in prescribed wave fields. Our results demonstrate that REs undergo scattering to large pitch angles and exhibit anomalous diffusion in both pitch-angle and kinetic energy space. Crucially, we observe a transition between diffusive, sub-diffusive, and super-diffusive transport regimes as a function of initial RE energy - an effect not captured by existing quasi-linear models. This anomalous transport behavior represents a significant advancement in understanding RE dynamics in the presence of wave - particle interactions. By identifying the conditions under which anomalous diffusion arises, this work lays the theoretical foundation for designing targeted, wave-based mitigation strategies in future tokamak experiments."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.13271","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:59:53Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:59:54Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-22T04:42:59Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12884","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12884","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12884","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pratik","familyName":"Nag","name":"Nag, Pratik","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andrew","familyName":"Zammit-Mangion","name":"Zammit-Mangion, Andrew","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ying","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Ying","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Modeling nonstationary spatial processes with normalizing flows"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Methodology (stat.ME)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (stat.ML)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T09:37:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:36:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-19T04:32:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-20T00:32:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-21T03:32:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-09-22T01:33:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Nonstationary spatial processes can often be represented as stationary processes on a warped spatial domain. Selecting an appropriate spatial warping function for a given application is often difficult and, as a result of this, warping methods have largely been limited to two-dimensional spatial domains. In this paper, we introduce a novel approach to modeling nonstationary, anisotropic spatial processes using neural autoregressive flows (NAFs), a class of invertible mappings capable of generating complex, high-dimensional warpings. Through simulation studies we demonstrate that a NAF-based model has greater representational capacity than other commonly used spatial process models. We apply our proposed modeling framework to a subset of the 3D Argo Floats dataset, highlighting the utility of our framework in real-world applications."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12884","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:50:50Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:50:50Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-22T04:42:59Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.13263","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.13263","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.13263","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yangjun","familyName":"Pu","name":"Pu, Yangjun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenyang","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Chenyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bohang","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Bohang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huigen","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Huigen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"The Fate of Exomoons Around Hot Jupiters"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Earth and Planetary Astrophysics (astro-ph.EP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T17:19:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:57:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-09-17T04:17:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-18T00:27:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Despite extensive searches, no exomoon has been confirmed. Cold gas giants are expected to host moons, while hot Jupiters (HJs), which migrate inward from distant formation sites, provide dynamically hostile environments for moons. In this paper, we systematically investigate the ultimate fate of exomoons around gas giants undergoing two primary migration mechanisms: disk migration and coplanar high-eccentricity migration (CHEM). Through $N$-body simulations using REBOUND, we find that during disk migration, retrograde moons exhibit a significantly higher survival fraction ($\\sim 25\\%$) compared to prograde ones ($\\sim 8\\%$). Conversely, for systems that successfully evolve into hot Jupiters via CHEM, no moons can survive the extreme orbital excitation; they are inevitably engulfed or gravitationally stripped before the inward migration. Interestingly, massive exomoons ($\\sim 10\\,M_{\\oplus}$) can exert a strong dynamical feedback on the host planet, acting as a dynamical shield that completely halts the high-eccentricity migration process to form HJs in $\\sim 30\\%$ of cases, preserving the satellite system. Furthermore, during chaotic multi-body interactions, $\\sim 10\\%$ of gas giants are dynamically ejected as free-floating planets (FFPs), with roughly $40\\%$ of these ejected planets successfully retaining their moons. Combining these dynamical instabilities with severe physical barriers such as atmospheric stripping and magnetic torques, we conclude that mature hot Jupiters are generally devoid of observable primordial moons. Consequently, future observational missions aiming to detect exomoons should focus on cold Jupiters and FFPs, which offer a more stable environment for moon retention."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"15 pages, 9 figures. Submitted to MNRAS"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.13263","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:59:42Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:59:42Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-18T02:44:31Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12390","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12390","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12390","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Evangelos","familyName":"Psomiadis","name":"Psomiadis, Evangelos","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Panagiotis","familyName":"Tsiotras","name":"Tsiotras, Panagiotis","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Distributed Event-Triggered Distance-Based Formation Control for Multi-Agent Systems"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T19:32:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:05:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-03T20:55:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-07T00:08:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-16T20:10:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-09-18T00:10:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This paper addresses collaborative formation control for multi-agent systems with limited resources. We consider a team of robots tasked with achieving a desired formation from an arbitrary initial configuration. To reduce unnecessary control updates and conserve resources, we propose a distributed event-triggered formation controller. Unlike the well-studied linear formation controllers, the proposed controller is nonlinear and relies on inter-agent distance measurements. Control updates are triggered only when the measurement error exceeds a state-dependent threshold, ensuring system stability while reducing actuation effort. We also employ a distributed control barrier function to guarantee inter-agent collision avoidance. The proposed controller is validated through simulations and real-world experiments involving different communication topologies, scalability tests, and variations in design parameters, and is also compared to periodic triggering strategies. Results demonstrate that the event-triggered approach significantly reduces control effort while preserving formation performance."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"6 pages, 6 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12390","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:39:20Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:39:21Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-18T02:44:30Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12626","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12626","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12626","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tao","familyName":"Long","name":"Long, Tao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuanming","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Xuanming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sitong","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Sitong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhou","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Zhou","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lydia B","familyName":"Chilton","name":"Chilton, Lydia B","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"DoubleAgents: Human-Agent Alignment in a Socially Embedded Workflow"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computers and Society (cs.CY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Emerging Technologies (cs.ET)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T03:43:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:19:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-28T16:42:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-29T01:57:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-06T16:19:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-04-07T01:37:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-09-14T18:33:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-09-16T00:05:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Aligning agentic AI with user intent is critical for delegating complex, socially embedded tasks, yet user preferences are often implicit, evolving, and difficult to specify upfront. We present DoubleAgents, a system for human-agent alignment in coordination tasks, grounded in distributed cognition. DoubleAgents integrates three components: (1) a coordination agent that maintains state and proposes plans and actions, (2) a dashboard visualization that makes the agent's reasoning legible for user evaluation, and (3) a policy module that transforms user edits into reusable alignment artifacts, including coordination policies, email templates, and stop hooks, which improve system behavior over time. We evaluate DoubleAgents through a two-day in-lab interactive simulation study (n=10), three real-world deployments, and a technical evaluation. Participants' comfort in offloading tasks and reliance on DoubleAgents both increased over time, correlating with the three distributed cognition components. Participants still required control at points of uncertainty - edge-case flagging and context-dependent actions. We contribute a distributed cognition approach to human-agent alignment in socially embedded tasks. We further introduce interactive simulation as a methodological testbed for rapid iteration and alignment testing of agentic systems."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"22 pages, 6 figures. ACM Conference on Human-AI Complementarity and Alignment (HCOMP 2026)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12626","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:44:46Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:44:47Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-16T02:40:21Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12429","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12429","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12429","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Valery","familyName":"Alexeev","name":"Alexeev, Valery","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alexander","familyName":"Kuznetsov","name":"Kuznetsov, Alexander","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Augmentations, reduced ideal point gluings and compact type degenerations of curves"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Algebraic Geometry (math.AG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T20:32:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:07:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-06T18:03:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-07T01:55:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-14T15:15:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-09-15T02:12:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In this note we demonstrate some unexpected properties that simple gluings of the simplest derived categories may have. We consider two special cases: the first is an augmented curve, i.e., the gluing of the derived categories of a point and a curve with the gluing bimodule given by the structure sheaf of the curve; the second is an ideal point gluing of curves, i.e., the gluing of the derived categories of two curves with the gluing bimodule given by the ideal sheaf of a point in the product of the curves. We construct unexpected exceptional objects contained in these categories and discuss their orthogonal complements.\n We also show that the simplest example of compact type degeneration of curves, a flat family of curves with a smooth general fiber and a 1-nodal reducible central fiber, gives rise to a smooth and proper family of triangulated categories with the general fiber an augmented curve and the central fiber the orthogonal complement of the exotic exceptional object in the ideal point gluing of curves, called the reduced ideal point gluing of curves."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"v3: accepted version, 33 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12429","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:40:13Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:40:14Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-15T04:41:22Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12422","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12422","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12422","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Robert D.","familyName":"Orlando","name":"Orlando, Robert D.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David S.","familyName":"Koltick","name":"Koltick, David S.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dennis E.","familyName":"Krause","name":"Krause, Dennis E.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Search for Quintessence-Like Pseudoscalar Dark Energy Effects on $^{56}\\text{Fe}$ Nuclear Transition Energies in Supernova 1991T"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cosmology and Nongalactic Astrophysics (astro-ph.CO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"High Energy Physics - Phenomenology (hep-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Nuclear Experiment (nucl-ex)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T20:19:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:07:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-09-11T20:12:00Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-15T00:12:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The nature of dark energy remains one of the most important unanswered problems in physics. Here we use observations of the Type Ia supernova 1991T to constrain the recent evolution of a dynamical pseudoscalar quintessence-like field $Q(t)$ by comparing the gamma ray spectra emitted by the $^{56}\\text{Fe}$ nuclei observed by COMPTEL aboard the Compton Gamma Ray Observatory to terrestrial values. We found that the average fractional energy shift of both the first and second excited states is $δE/E = -0.006\\pm0.008$, including statistical and systematic errors, indicating that the energies of these astrophysical gamma rays are consistent with the gamma rays produced in terrestrial labs. Assuming that any energy shift is caused by a dynamical QCD axion-like pseudoscalar field $Q(t)$, the observed energy deviations are consistent with a fractional rate of change of the pion mass at the $68\\%$ Gaussian limit given by $ 3\\times10^{-11} \\geq \\dot{m_π}/m_π\\geq-15\\times10^{-11}\\text{ yr}^{-1}$. The observed energy deviation was also used to determine the rate of change of the quintessence-like field ($\\dot{Q}_0$) for tracking models: $ -1\\times10^7\\leq \\dot{Q}_{\\rm 0} \\leq 7\\times10^7 \\text{ GeV/yr}$. This upper limit on $\\dot{Q}_0$ results in a fractional kinetic energy $Ω_{KE}\\leq0.029$ and equation of state $-1.0\\leq w_{Q}\\leq-0.92$, which are consistent with the cosmological constant ($\\dot{Q}_0 =0$, $Ω_{KE}=0$, and $w=-1.0$). Finally, these results were complemented by cosmological observations to place limits on the tracker model decay constant coefficient and tracking power potential."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12422","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:40:03Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:40:04Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-15T04:41:21Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12386","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12386","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12386","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Asim","familyName":"Waheed","name":"Waheed, Asim","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vasisht","familyName":"Duddu","name":"Duddu, Vasisht","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sebastian","familyName":"Szyller","name":"Szyller, Sebastian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Amulet: a Python Library for Assessing Interactions Among ML Defenses and Risks"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T19:27:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:05:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-07T14:22:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-10T01:42:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-09T23:53:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-09-11T00:15:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Machine learning (ML) models are susceptible to various risks to security, privacy, and fairness. Most defenses are designed to protect against each risk individually (intended interactions) but can inadvertently affect susceptibility to other unrelated risks (unintended interactions). We introduce Amulet, the first Python library for evaluating both intended and unintended interactions among ML defenses and risks. Amulet is comprehensive by including representative attacks, defenses, and metrics; extensible to new modules due to its modular design; consistent with a user-friendly API template for inputs and outputs; and applicable for evaluating novel interactions. By satisfying all four properties, Amulet offers a unified foundation for studying how defenses interact, enabling the first systematic evaluation of unintended interactions across multiple risks."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"10 pages, 4 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12386","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:39:15Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:39:16Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-11T02:36:52Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12641","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12641","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12641","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"So","familyName":"Katagiri","name":"Katagiri, So","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yoshiki","familyName":"Matsuoka","name":"Matsuoka, Yoshiki","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Akio","familyName":"Sugamoto","name":"Sugamoto, Akio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Applications of Nambu Non-equilibrium Thermodynamics to Specific Phenomena"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Statistical Mechanics (cond-mat.stat-mech)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"High Energy Physics - Theory (hep-th)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T03:57:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:21:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-29T02:10:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-30T00:20:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-05T03:46:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-01-06T01:57:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-02-28T15:03:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-03-03T01:43:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-06-16T06:47:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:32:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2026-06-22T00:53:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v6"},{"date":"2026-06-23T02:07:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v6"},{"date":"2026-07-30T06:11:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v7"},{"date":"2026-07-31T00:28:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v7"},{"date":"2026-09-09T03:45:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v8"},{"date":"2026-09-10T00:22:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v8"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"8","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We apply Nambu non-equilibrium thermodynamics (NNET), a dynamics with multiple Hamiltonians coupled to entropy-induced dissipation, to paradigmatic far-from-equilibrium systems. Concretely, we construct NNET realizations for the Belousov-Zhabotinsky (BZ) reaction (oscillatory), the Hindmarsh-Rose neuron model (spiking), and the Lorenz and Chen systems (chaotic), and analyze their dynamical and thermodynamic signatures. Across all cases the velocity field cleanly decomposes into a non-dissipative Nambu part and a dissipative entropy-gradient part, anchored by a model-independent quasi-conserved quantity. This construction reproduces cycles, spikes, and strange-attractor behavior. These results demonstrate that NNET provides a unified, quantitatively consistent framework for oscillatory, spiking, and chaotic non-equilibrium systems, offering a systematic description beyond the Onsager-type near-equilibrium linear-response framework and complementary to nonlinear geometric formulations such as GENERIC."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"39 pages and 27 figures, v2 This paper has undergone a comprehensive revision to enhance its overall clarity, coherence, and readability for the reader, v3, v4, v5 added some explanations, v6 updated the references, v7 added some explanations, figures, and references, v8 organized wordings and contents"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12641","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":7,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:45:07Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:45:08Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-10T02:28:01Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12635","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12635","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12635","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sheng","familyName":"Shen","name":"Shen, Sheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rémi","familyName":"Munos","name":"Munos, Rémi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongyuan","familyName":"Zhan","name":"Zhan, Hongyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuandong","familyName":"Tian","name":"Tian, Yuandong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Positional Encoding via Token-Aware Phase Attention"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T03:53:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T16:56:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-25T21:11:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-29T00:10:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-10T22:28:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-05-12T01:30:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-09-06T17:50:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-09-09T01:09:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Zero v1.0 Universal","rightsIdentifier":"cc0-1.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We prove under practical assumptions that Rotary Positional Embedding (RoPE) introduces an intrinsic distance-dependent bias in attention scores that limits RoPE's ability to model long-context. RoPE extension methods may alleviate this issue, but they typically require post-hoc adjustments after pretraining, such as rescaling or hyperparameters retuning. This paper introduces Token-Aware Phase Attention (TAPA), a new positional encoding method that incorporates a learnable phase function into the attention mechanism. TAPA preserves token interactions over long range, extends to longer contexts with direct and light continual pretraining, extrapolates to unseen lengths, and attains substantially lower perplexity and stronger retrieval performance in the long-context regime than RoPE-style baselines."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"28 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12635","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":4,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:44:59Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:44:59Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-09T05:53:55Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.13073","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.13073","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.13073","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuki","familyName":"Mizuno","name":"Mizuno, Yuki","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Dualizing complexes over $\\mathbb{Z}$-algebras"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Rings and Algebras (math.RA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Algebraic Geometry (math.AG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Category Theory (math.CT)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"14A22, 16S38","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T13:29:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:47:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T09:39:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-18T00:35:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-04T15:24:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-09-07T00:56:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In this paper, we introduce the notions of dualizing complexes and balanced dualizing complexes over $\\mathbb{Z}$-algebras. We prove that a noetherian connected $\\mathbb{Z}$-algebra $A$ admits a balanced dualizing complex if and only if $A$ satisfies Artin-Zhang's $χ$-condition, has finite local cohomology dimension, and possesses symmetric derived torsion as a bigraded $A$-$A$-bimodule. As an application of our study of dualizing complexes, we show that any smooth noncommutative projective scheme associated to a $\\mathbb{Z}$-algebra with a balanced dualizing complex admits a Serre functor."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"47 pages. Comments are welcome"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.13073","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:55:11Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:55:12Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-07T02:15:09Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12596","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12596","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12596","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiasong","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Jiasong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Linchen","familyName":"Qian","name":"Qian, Linchen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruonan","familyName":"Gong","name":"Gong, Ruonan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Christina","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Christina","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tongran","familyName":"Qin","name":"Qin, Tongran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Thuy","familyName":"Pham","name":"Pham, Thuy","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Caitlin","familyName":"Martin","name":"Martin, Caitlin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohammad","familyName":"Zafar","name":"Zafar, Mohammad","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"John","familyName":"Elefteriades","name":"Elefteriades, John","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liang","familyName":"Liang","name":"Liang, Liang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Computational Pipeline for Patient-Specific Modeling of Thoracic Aortic Aneurysm: From Medical Image to Finite Element Analysis"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Image and Video Processing (eess.IV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computational Engineering, Finance, and Science (cs.CE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T02:50:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:17:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-09-04T17:02:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-07T01:02:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The aorta is the body's largest arterial vessel, serving as the primary pathway for oxygenated blood within the systemic circulation. Aortic aneurysms consistently rank among the top twenty causes of mortality in the United States. Thoracic aortic aneurysm (TAA) arises from abnormal dilation of the thoracic aorta and remains a clinically significant disease, ranking as one of the leading causes of death in adults. A thoracic aortic aneurysm ruptures when the integrity of all aortic wall layers is compromised due to elevated blood pressure. Currently, three-dimensional computed tomography (3D CT) is considered the gold standard for diagnosing TAA. The geometric characteristics of the aorta, which can be quantified from medical imaging, and stresses on the aortic wall, which can be obtained by finite element analysis (FEA), are critical in evaluating the risk of rupture and dissection. Deep learning based image segmentation has emerged as a reliable method for extracting anatomical regions of interest from medical images. Voxel based segmentation masks of anatomical structures are typically converted into structured mesh representation to enable accurate simulation. Hexahedral meshes are commonly used in finite element simulations of the aorta due to their computational efficiency and superior simulation accuracy. Due to anatomical variability, patient specific modeling enables detailed assessment of individual anatomical and biomechanics behaviors, supporting precise simulations, accurate diagnoses, and personalized treatment strategies. Finite element (FE) simulations provide valuable insights into the biomechanical behaviors of tissues and organs in clinical studies. Developing accurate FE models represents a crucial initial step in establishing a patient-specific, biomechanically based framework for predicting the risk of TAA."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12596","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:44:04Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:44:05Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-07T02:15:08Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12370","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12370","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12370","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bikun","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Bikun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daniel","familyName":"Dilley","name":"Dilley, Daniel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alvin","familyName":"Gonzales","name":"Gonzales, Alvin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Thomas A.","familyName":"Hahn","name":"Hahn, Thomas A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ryan","familyName":"White","name":"White, Ryan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rotem","familyName":"Arnon","name":"Arnon, Rotem","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hannes","familyName":"Bernien","name":"Bernien, Hannes","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zain","familyName":"Saleem","name":"Saleem, Zain","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liang","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Liang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Efficient Entanglement Purification Circuit Design for Dual-Species Atom Arrays"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Quantum Physics (quant-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T19:04:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:04:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-13T01:22:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-14T01:20:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-03T23:30:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-09-07T00:12:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1103/6j15-7mty","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Entanglement purification protocols (EPPs) are essential for generating high-fidelity entangled states in noisy quantum systems, enabling robust quantum networking and computation. Building on the circuit of the foundational recurrence protocol, we generalize two-way EPPs to arbitrary stabilizer codes. Through analytical derivations and noisy circuit simulations incorporating circuit-level noise, we demonstrate enhanced purification performance, with fidelity improvements and finite distillation rates for distillable input states. We propose efficient circuit designs for EPPs tailored to dual-species Rydberg atom arrays, leveraging species-specific laser control and interspecies Rydberg interactions. Introducing a low-overhead operation set, the dual-species atom convenient operation set, we facilitate straightforward compilation of EPP circuits without the need for ancillary atoms or complex atom rearrangements. Our framework provides practical guidance for near-term implementations on dual-species platforms, advancing towards scalable entanglement distribution in neutral atom systems and paving the way for fault-tolerant quantum technologies."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"20 pages, 10 figures. Revised with corrections and clarifications following journal publication"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12370","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-09-17T01:38:53Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:38:54Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-07T02:15:07Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12338","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12338","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12338","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"A. de Oliveira","familyName":"Junior","name":"Junior, A. de Oliveira","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anton L.","familyName":"Andersen","name":"Andersen, Anton L.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Benjamin Lundgren","familyName":"Larsen","name":"Larsen, Benjamin Lundgren","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sean William","familyName":"Moore","name":"Moore, Sean William","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Damian","familyName":"Markham","name":"Markham, Damian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Masahiro","familyName":"Takeoka","name":"Takeoka, Masahiro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jonatan Bohr","familyName":"Brask","name":"Brask, Jonatan Bohr","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ulrik L.","familyName":"Andersen","name":"Andersen, Ulrik L.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Privacy in continuous-variable distributed quantum sensing"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Quantum Physics (quant-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T18:06:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:02:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-09-04T09:47:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-07T00:39:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Can a distributed network of quantum sensors estimate a global parameter while protecting every locally encoded value? We answer this question affirmatively by introducing and analysing a protocol for distributed quantum sensing in the continuous-variable regime. We consider a multipartite network in which an unknown local phase is imprinted at each node on a shared entangled Gaussian state. We show that the average phase can be estimated with high precision, exhibiting Heisenberg scaling in the total photon number, while individual phases are inaccessible. We further prove a no-go theorem showing that, for three or more parties, no finite-energy Gaussian probe can provide complete privacy of the average phase, meaning that all phase combinations orthogonal to the average remain entirely hidden. This identifies the two-party case as an exceptional Gaussian setting that can achieve complete privacy. We further investigate the impact of displacements and optical losses, revealing trade-offs between estimation accuracy and privacy. Finally, we benchmark the protocol against other continuous-variable resource states."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"25 pages, 8 figures. Published version"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12338","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:38:10Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:38:10Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-07T02:15:07Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12545","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12545","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12545","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vijay","familyName":"Kumar","name":"Kumar, Vijay","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kaitlyn M.","familyName":"Mullin","name":"Mullin, Kaitlyn M.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hyunggon","familyName":"Park","name":"Park, Hyunggon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Matthew","familyName":"Gerigk","name":"Gerigk, Matthew","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andrew","familyName":"Bresk","name":"Bresk, Andrew","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tresa M.","familyName":"Pollock","name":"Pollock, Tresa M.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yangying","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Yangying","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Coupled Infrared Imaging and Multiphysics Modeling to Predict Three-Dimensional Thermal Characteristics during Selective Laser Melting"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Materials Science (cond-mat.mtrl-sci)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T00:54:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-09-04T01:10:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1038/s43246-026-01281-z","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Laser heating during additive manufacturing (AM) induces extreme and transient thermal conditions which critically influence the microstructure evolution and mechanical properties of the resulting component. However, accurately resolving these conditions with sufficient spatiotemporal accuracy remains a central challenge. We demonstrate a unique approach that couples high-speed infrared imaging, during selective laser melting of MAR-M247, with a transient three-dimensional (3D) multiphysics simulation to reconstruct the dynamic sub-surface temperature distribution of the melt pool. This integrated framework enables the estimation of experimentally-validated, 3D solidification conditions-including solidification velocities and cooling rates-at the solid-liquid interface while also significantly lowering computational cost. By quantifying solidification conditions, we predict variations in microstructure size and orientation driven by laser processing parameters and validate them with ex situ scanning electron microscopy and electron backscatter diffraction maps. Our findings substantiate that an integrated experimental-computational approach is crucial to realize in situ prediction and optimization of microstructures in commercial AM."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"28 pages, 7 Figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12545","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-09-17T01:42:53Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:42:53Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-04T02:37:20Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12417","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12417","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12417","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Christopher","familyName":"Boyd","name":"Boyd, Christopher","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vinícius","familyName":"Miranda","name":"Miranda, Vinícius","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Lattice isomorphic Banach lattices of polynomials"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Functional Analysis (math.FA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T20:13:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:07:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-03T12:33:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-04T01:57:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-01T12:26:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-09-02T01:00:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We study Díaz-Dineen's problem for regular homogeneous vector-valued polynomials. In particular, we prove that if $E^*$ and $F^*$ are lattice isomorphic\n with at least one having order continuous norm, then $\\mathcal{P}^r(^n E;\n G^*)$ and $\\mathcal{P}^r(^n F; G^*)$ are lattice isomorphic for every $n\\in\n \\N$ and every Banach lattice $G$. We also study\n the analogous problem for the classes of regular\n compact, regular weakly compact, orthogonally additive\n and regular nuclear polynomials."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"22 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12417","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:39:56Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:39:57Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-02T02:59:16Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12584","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12584","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12584","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiguo","familyName":"Liang","name":"Liang, Yiguo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanjun","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Yanjun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Sharp mean-field analysis of permutation mixtures and permutation-invariant decisions"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Statistics Theory (math.ST)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Information Theory (cs.IT)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T02:22:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:16:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-31T05:06:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-01T01:35:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We develop sharp bounds on the statistical distance between high-dimensional permutation mixtures and their i.i.d. counterparts. Our approach establishes a new geometric link between the spectrum of a complex channel overlap matrix and the information geometry of the channel, yielding tight dimension-independent bounds that close gaps left by previous work. Within this geometric framework, we also derive dimension-dependent bounds that uncover phase transitions in dimensionality for Gaussian and Poisson families. Applied to compound decision problems, this refined control of permutation mixtures enables sharper mean-field analyses of permutation-invariant decision rules, yielding strong non-asymptotic equivalence results between two notions of compound regret in Gaussian and Poisson models."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"To appear in IEEE Transactions on Information Theory"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12584","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:43:47Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:43:48Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-01T05:00:49Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12458","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12458","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12458","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Àlmos","familyName":"Veres-Vitàlyos","name":"Veres-Vitàlyos, Àlmos","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Filip","familyName":"Lemic","name":"Lemic, Filip","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daniel Johannes","familyName":"Bugelnig","name":"Bugelnig, Daniel Johannes","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Joan Bernaus","familyName":"Casadesús","name":"Casadesús, Joan Bernaus","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Genis Castillo","familyName":"Gomez-Raya","name":"Gomez-Raya, Genis Castillo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sergi","familyName":"Abadal","name":"Abadal, Sergi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bernhard","familyName":"Rinner","name":"Rinner, Bernhard","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xavier","familyName":"Costa-Pérez","name":"Costa-Pérez, Xavier","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Neural 3D Object Reconstruction with Small-Scale Unmanned Aerial Vehicles"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Hardware Architecture (cs.AR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Emerging Technologies (cs.ET)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Systems and Control (eess.SY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T21:08:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:09:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-21T14:13:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-22T00:56:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-30T10:26:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-09-01T01:02:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Miniaturized Uncrewed Aerial Vehicles (UAVs) can access indoor and hard-to-reach spaces, but severe constraints on payload and autonomy have limited their use in demanding tasks such as high-quality 3D reconstruction. We introduce a novel system architecture that enables autonomous, high-fidelity 3D scanning of static objects with sub-100 gram UAVs. Our core innovation lies in a closed-loop active viewpoint selection framework specifically tailored for ultra-constrained micro-platforms, advancing beyond standard static or offline active reconstruction methods. The framework establishes a dual-reconstruction pipeline that creates a real-time (RT) feedback loop between data capture and flight control. A near-RT process uses Structure-from-Motion (SfM) to generate an instantaneous point-cloud of the object. A systematic trajectory adaptation algorithm analyzes the model quality on the fly and dynamically adapts the UAV's trajectory based on parameterized spatial partitioning to intelligently capture new images of poorly covered areas, ensuring comprehensive acquisition. For the final, high-fidelity output, a non-RT pipeline employs a Neural Radiance Fields (NeRF)-based Neural 3D Reconstruction (N3DR) approach, fusing SfM-derived camera poses with precise external location data, evaluated across both radio-based Ultra Wideband (UWB) and visual motion-capture setups, to correct sensor noise and achieve superior accuracy. We implemented and validated this architecture using Crazyflie 2.1 UAVs. Our experiments, conducted in both single- and multi-UAV configurations show that algorithmic dynamic trajectory adaptation consistently improves reconstruction quality over static flight paths. This work demonstrates a scalable and autonomous solution that unlocks the potential of miniaturized UAVs for fine-grained 3D reconstruction, a capability previously reserved for much larger platforms."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"14 pages, 18 figures, 4 tables, 44 references"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12458","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:40:53Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:40:54Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-01T05:00:48Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12956","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12956","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12956","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Diogo","familyName":"Boito","name":"Boito, Diogo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marcelle","familyName":"Caram","name":"Caram, Marcelle","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Perturbative QCD below charm threshold: theory and tensions with $e^+e^-$ data"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"High Energy Physics - Phenomenology (hep-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"High Energy Physics - Experiment (hep-ex)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T11:00:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:40:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-13T14:04:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-01T01:12:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-25T11:58:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-08-26T00:53:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1103/dtg5-ghdx","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In this paper we carefully assess the theory prediction for $R(s)$ below charm threshold, $R_{uds}$, and address tensions with the existing data, notably with the 2021 BES-III results. We analyze the uncertainty of the perturbative QCD description in the light of renormalons making use of the large-$β_0$ limit and the renormalon-free gluon-condensate scheme. We provide a reliable estimate of the duality violation contributions; we show they are sizable up to $2.5$~GeV and improve the agreement between theory and data, but are negligible for higher energies. We then combine the available experimental data for $R_{uds}$ and find the data sets to be mutually compatible. Finally, we compare theory and data, both locally and in their contributions to the anomalous magnetic moment of the muon. Theory is compatible with the combined data but discrepancies with the BES-III data reach more than 3$σ$."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"35 pages, 6 figures. Corrected quark-mass contribution in Eq. (32). Conclusions unchanged; no impact on main results"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12956","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-09-17T01:52:29Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:52:30Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-26T02:36:34Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.13267","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.13267","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.13267","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alexander","familyName":"Dombowsky","name":"Dombowsky, Alexander","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David B.","familyName":"Dunson","name":"Dunson, David B.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Learning discrete Bayesian networks with hierarchical Dirichlet shrinkage"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Methodology (stat.ME)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (stat.ML)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T17:24:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:58:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-27T21:52:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-29T00:11:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-23T16:25:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-08-25T01:01:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"A discrete Bayesian network is a directed acyclic graph (DAG) consisting of categorical variables. Two popular approaches for DBN modeling include classification and nonparametric methods. However, both methods often require a large number of parameters, such as high-order interactions in the former and cell probabilities in the latter. In this article, we propose a hierarchical model for node-parent conditional probabilities, inducing shrinkage to low-dimensional latent parameters aposteriori. We generate samples from the posterior distribution of these latent variables using the Metropolis-adjusted Langevin algorithm within a Gibbs sampler. Moreover, we verify that the full conditional distribution is log-concave under mild conditions, facilitating efficient sampling. We then detail several algorithms for structure learning that incorporate our hierarchical prior and preserve the DAG property. Through simulations, we evaluate the performance of our method for sparse counts, discovering graph structure, and selecting between competing DAGs. We conclude with an application to uncovering prognostic network structure from a breast cancer dataset."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.13267","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:59:48Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:59:48Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-25T03:49:47Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12794","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12794","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12794","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yang","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhijun","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Zhijun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"The Impact of Automation on Risk-Taking: The Role of Sense of Agency"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T08:12:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-25T00:48:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1016/j.actpsy.2026.107681","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Automation significantly alters human behavior, particularly risk-taking. Previous researches have paid limited attention to the underlying characteristics of automation and their mechanisms of influence on risk-taking. This study investigated how automation affects risk-taking and examined the role of sense of agency therein. By quantifying sense of agency through subjective ratings, this research explored the impact of automation level and reliability level on risk-taking. The results of three experiments indicated that automation reduced the level of risk-taking; higher automation level was associated with lower sense of agency and lower risk-taking, with sense of agency playing a complete mediating role; higher automation reliability was associated with higher sense of agency and higher risk-taking, with sense of agency playing a partial mediating role. The study concludes that automation influences risk-taking, such that higher automation level or lower reliability is associated with a lower likelihood of risk-taking. Sense of agency mediates the impact of automation on risk-taking, and automation level and reliability have different effects on risk-taking."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"37 pages, 9 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12794","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-09-17T01:48:39Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:48:39Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-25T03:49:46Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12811","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12811","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12811","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Binquan","familyName":"Ji","name":"Ji, Binquan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaqi","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Jiaqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruiting","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Ruiting","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xingchen","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Xingchen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiyang","familyName":"Qi","name":"Qi, Yiyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shichao","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Shichao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yifei","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Yifei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuantao","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Yuantao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Feiliang","familyName":"Ren","name":"Ren, Feiliang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ConvergeWriter: Data-Driven Bottom-Up Article Construction"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T08:30:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:32:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-23T10:53:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-25T00:54:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) have shown remarkable prowess in text generation, yet producing long-form, factual documents grounded in extensive external knowledge bases remains a significant challenge. Existing \"top-down\" methods, which first generate a hypothesis or outline and then retrieve evidence, often suffer from a disconnect between the model's plan and the available knowledge, leading to content fragmentation and factual inaccuracies. To address these limitations, we propose a novel \"bottom-up,\" data-driven framework that inverts the conventional generation pipeline. Our approach is predicated on a \"Retrieval-First for Knowledge, Clustering for Structure\" strategy, which first establishes the \"knowledge boundaries\" of the source corpus before any generative planning occurs. Specifically, we perform exhaustive iterative retrieval from the knowledge base and then employ an unsupervised clustering algorithm to organize the retrieved documents into distinct \"knowledge clusters.\" These clusters form an objective, data-driven foundation that directly guides the subsequent generation of a hierarchical outline and the final document content. This bottom-up process ensures that the generated text is strictly constrained by and fully traceable to the source material, proactively adapting to the finite scope of the knowledge base and fundamentally mitigating the risk of hallucination. Experimental results on both 14B and 32B parameter models demonstrate that our method achieves performance comparable to or exceeding state-of-the-art baselines, and is expected to demonstrate unique advantages in knowledge-constrained scenarios that demand high fidelity and structural coherence. Our work presents an effective paradigm for generating reliable, structured, long-form documents, paving the way for more robust LLM applications in high-stakes, knowledge-intensive domains."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12811","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:49:07Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:49:08Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-25T03:49:46Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12587","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12587","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12587","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qizhai","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Qizhai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peng","familyName":"Ding","name":"Ding, Peng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Inverse regression for causal inference with multiple outcomes"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Methodology (stat.ME)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T02:26:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:16:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-20T07:40:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-21T00:29:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"With multiple outcomes in empirical research, a common strategy is to define a composite outcome as a weighted average of the original outcomes. However, the choices of weights are often subjective and can be controversial. We propose an inverse regression strategy for causal inference with multiple outcomes. The key idea is to regress the treatment on the outcomes, which is the inverse of the standard regression of the outcomes on the treatment. Although this strategy is simple and even counterintuitive, it has several advantages. First, testing for zero coefficients of the outcomes is equivalent to testing for zero treatment effects, even though the inverse regression is deemed misspecified. Second, the coefficients of the outcomes provide data-driven weights for defining a composite outcome. Interestingly, these weights maximize the standardized effect size, a measure commonly used for univariate outcomes in empirical studies. We also discuss the associated inference issues. Third, this strategy is applicable to general study designs. We illustrate the theory in both randomized experiments and observational studies. We implement the proposed method in our R package invreg, available at https://github.com/zhangwei0125/invreg."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"80 pages, 5 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12587","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:43:51Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:43:52Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-21T02:22:14Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12407","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12407","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12407","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alessio","familyName":"Catanzaro","name":"Catanzaro, Alessio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rajat Subhra","familyName":"Hazra","name":"Hazra, Rajat Subhra","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Diego","familyName":"Garlaschelli","name":"Garlaschelli, Diego","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Spectra of random graphs with discrete scale invariance"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Spectral Theory (math.SP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Disordered Systems and Neural Networks (cond-mat.dis-nn)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Statistical Mechanics (cond-mat.stat-mech)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Mathematical Physics (math-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Probability (math.PR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T19:53:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-18T00:30:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-20T15:44:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-21T00:55:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Random graphs defined by an occurrence probability that is invariant under node aggregation have been identified recently in the context of network renormalization. The invariance property requires that edges are drawn with a specific probability that, in the annealed case, depends on a necessarily infinite-mean node fitness. The diverging mean determines many properties that are uncommon in models with independent edges, but at the same time widespread in real-world networks. Here we focus on the leading eigenvalues and eigenvectors of the adjacency matrix of the model, where the n nodes are assigned a Pareto($α$)-distributed fitness with 0 \u0026lt; $α$ \u0026lt; 1. We find that the leading eigenvalues are all of order square root of n, alternate in sign and are located at the intersection between the real axis and a logarithmic spiral in the complex plane, which we characterize analytically in terms of the Gamma function. We also calculate the associated eigenvectors, finding that they display complexvalued scaling exponents and log-periodicity, which are signatures of discrete scale invariance. In contrast with the typical finite-rank behaviour of random graphs with finite-mean variables, we find that a growing number of the leading eigenvalues emerges from the bulk, whose edge extends up to order square root of n and therefore reaches the same scale as that of the structural eigenvalues."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12407","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:39:43Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:39:44Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-21T02:22:13Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.13129","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.13129","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.13129","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"A. G.","familyName":"Magner","name":"Magner, A. G.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"S. P.","familyName":"Maydanyuk","name":"Maydanyuk, S. P.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"A.","familyName":"Bonasera","name":"Bonasera, A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"H.","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, H.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"S. N.","familyName":"Fedotkin","name":"Fedotkin, S. N.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"A. I.","familyName":"Levon","name":"Levon, A. I.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"T.","familyName":"Depastas","name":"Depastas, T.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"U. V.","familyName":"Grygoriev","name":"Grygoriev, U. V.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"A. A.","familyName":"Uleiev","name":"Uleiev, A. A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Rotating neutron stars within the macroscopic effective-surface approximation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"General Relativity and Quantum Cosmology (gr-qc)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"High Energy Astrophysical Phenomena (astro-ph.HE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Solar and Stellar Astrophysics (astro-ph.SR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Nuclear Theory (nucl-th)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"F.2","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T14:41:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:50:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-06T18:52:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-09T01:02:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-19T17:17:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-08-20T01:01:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The macroscopic model for a neutron star (NS) as a finite perfect fluid at the equilibrium is extended to rotating systems by incorporating the linear perturbation expansion over a small frequency $ω$ near Schwarzschild outer-inner gravitational metric within the effective-surface (ES) approach. The NS angular momentum $I$ and moment of inertia (MI) for a slow stationary azimuthal rotation around the symmetry axis are calculated by using the Kerr metric approach in spherical coordinates, and compared with Boyer-Lindquist (outer) and Hogan (inner) metric results. The volume and gradient-surface terms of the macroscopic NS energy density $\\mathcal{E}(ρ)$ (Equation of State) are taken into account at the leading order of the leptodermic parameter $a/R \\ll 1$, where $a$ is the ES crust thickness and $R$ is the NS effective radius. The analytical macroscopic NS MI expressions, $Θ= \\mathrm{d}I/\\mathrm{d}ω= \\tildeΘ/(1-\\mathcal{T}_{tφ})$, have been obtained in terms of the statistically averaged MI, $\\tildeΘ$, and its time and azimuthal-angle $t,φ$ correlation, $\\mathcal{T}_{tφ}$, as sums of the volume and surface components. The MI $Θ$ is changed significantly as function of the effective radius $R$ because of a strong gravity. We found the additional constraint for the NS radius to smaller accessible ranges which is due mainly to the $t,φ$ correlations and surface contributions. The adiabaticity conditions for applicability of the linear perturbation theory is carried out for several neutron stars with a strong gravity and relatively large rotation periods."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"34 pages, 13 figures, 3 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.13129","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:56:35Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:56:36Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-20T02:17:59Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12703","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12703","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12703","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gyungmin","familyName":"Cho","name":"Cho, Gyungmin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dohun","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Dohun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Sample-optimal single-copy quantum state tomography with shallow-depth measurements"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Quantum Physics (quant-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T05:54:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:25:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-07-26T10:28:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-28T00:50:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-17T18:31:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-08-19T00:04:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Quantum state tomography (QST) is a central task in quantum information, and its efficiency is commonly characterized by sample complexity. Although collective measurements on multiple copies achieve optimal performance, they are difficult to implement on near-term devices, motivating the study of single-copy approaches. Here, we introduce an ancilla-free single-copy QST protocol based on logarithmic-depth local circuits on an $n$-qubit system. For rank-$r$ states in dimension $d=2^n$, our protocol achieves trace-norm error $ε$ using $\\mathcal{O}(dr^2\\log d/ε^2)$ copies, matching the single-copy lower bound up to a logarithmic factor. For full-rank mixed states, it removes this logarithmic overhead and achieves the optimal scaling $\\mathcal{O}(d^3/ε^2)$, with nearly optimal classical runtime for explicit matrix output. These results show that sample-optimal QST can be realized using experimentally accessible shallow-depth measurements."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12703","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:46:32Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:46:33Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-19T02:23:45Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12757","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12757","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12757","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaohan","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Xiaohan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Si-Yuan","familyName":"Cao","name":"Cao, Si-Yuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaokai","familyName":"Bai","name":"Bai, Xiaokai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiming","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yiming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhangkai","familyName":"Shen","name":"Shen, Zhangkai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhe","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Zhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lun","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Lun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qi","familyName":"Ming","name":"Ming, Qi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoxi","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Xiaoxi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hui-liang","familyName":"Shen","name":"Shen, Hui-liang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Recurrent Cross-View Object Geo-Localization"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T07:18:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:29:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-16T10:40:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-18T00:53:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Cross-view object geo-localization (CVOGL) aims to determine the location of a specific object in high-resolution satellite imagery given a query image with a point prompt. Existing approaches treat CVOGL as a one-shot detection process, directly regressing object locations from cross-view information aggregation, but they are vulnerable to feature noise and lack mechanisms for error correction. In this paper, we propose ReCOT, a Recurrent Cross-view Object geo-localization Transformer, which models CVOGL as a recurrent localization process. ReCOT introduces a set of learnable tokens that encode task-specific intent from the query image and prompt embeddings, and iteratively attend to the reference features to refine the predicted location. To enhance this recurrent process, we incorporate two complementary modules: (1) a SAM-based knowledge distillation strategy that transfers segmentation priors from the Segment Anything Model (SAM) to provide clearer semantic guidance without additional inference cost, and (2) a Reference Feature Enhancement Module (RFEM) that introduces hierarchical attention to emphasize object-relevant regions in the reference features. Extensive experiments on CVOGL benchmarks demonstrate that ReCOT achieves state-of-the-art (SOTA) performance while significantly reducing parameters compared to previous SOTA approaches. Our code is available at https://github.com/Temperature-ai/ReCOT.git."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12757","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:47:47Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:47:48Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-18T03:56:13Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12261","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12261","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12261","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marko","familyName":"Djukanovic","name":"Djukanovic, Marko","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Christian","familyName":"Blum","name":"Blum, Christian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aleksandar","familyName":"Kartelj","name":"Kartelj, Aleksandar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ana","familyName":"Nikolikj","name":"Nikolikj, Ana","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guenther","familyName":"Raidl","name":"Raidl, Guenther","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Longest Filled Common Subsequence for Song Identification from Degraded Audio via Construct--Merge--Solve--Adapt Optimization"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Sound (cs.SD)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Audio and Speech Processing (eess.AS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T19:17:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:01:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T18:51:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-25T02:05:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-13T11:35:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-08-14T00:43:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This paper addresses the Longest Filled Common Subsequence (LFCS) problem, a challenging NP-hard problem with applications in bioinformatics, including gene mutation prediction and genomic data reconstruction. Existing approaches, including exact, metaheuristic, and approximation algorithms, have primarily been evaluated on small instances, which provide limited insight into their scalability. In this work, we introduce a new benchmark dataset with significantly larger instances and demonstrate that existing datasets lack the discriminative power needed to meaningfully assess algorithm performance at scale. To solve large instances efficiently, we utilize an adaptive Construct, Merge, Solve, Adapt (CMSA) framework that iteratively generates promising subproblems via component-based construction and refines them using feedback from prior iterations. Subproblems are solved using an external black-box solver. Extensive experiments on both standard and newly introduced set of large-sized instances prove that the designed adaptive CMSA achieves state-of-the-art performance, outperforming four known approaches. Notably, among 1,510 problem instances with known optimal solutions, our approach matches the proven optimum on 1,486 instances---corresponding to 98.4\\% of these instances. These results demonstrate substantially improved scalability on large problem instances. As an engineering contribution, we propose a novel application of LFCS to song identification from degraded audio excerpts, using real-world energy-profile instances derived from popular music. Finally, we conduct an empirical explainability analysis to identify critical feature combinations influencing algorithm performance, revealing the key problem features associated with the success or failure of the approaches across different instance types."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"60 pages, 9 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12261","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:36:24Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:36:24Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-14T02:22:39Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.13211","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.13211","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.13211","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Irene","familyName":"Testa","name":"Testa, Irene","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Luigi","familyName":"Quarantiello","name":"Quarantiello, Luigi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eric Nuertey","familyName":"Coleman","name":"Coleman, Eric Nuertey","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Samrat","familyName":"Mukherjee","name":"Mukherjee, Samrat","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Julio","familyName":"Hurtado","name":"Hurtado, Julio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vincenzo","familyName":"Lomonaco","name":"Lomonaco, Vincenzo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"GLAM: Efficient Continual Learning at Scale via Grouped LoRA Adapter Merging"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T16:18:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:55:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T15:02:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-18T00:48:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-18T13:56:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09-19T00:42:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-08-11T14:38:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-08-12T00:59:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The ability to learn continuously over time remains a major challenge for modern machine learning systems, even in the era of Foundation Models. While the rich representations learned by large pre-trained models can partially mitigate catastrophic forgetting, they still struggle to adapt efficiently to evolving data distributions. A key challenge remains, how to continually add new knowledge to a large pretrained model in a way that is scalable and computationally efficient over long task sequences. In this work, we introduce GLAM, a simple and effective framework for class-incremental continual learning based on LoRA adapters merging. For each task, GLAM trains a lightweight low-rank adapter with an importance scalar, incurring minimal computational overhead. Adapters are then pruned, rescaled, and sequentially grouped to enable structured knowledge reuse and limit parameter growth. At inference, all groups are combined into a single module, ensuring constant computational cost regardless of the number of tasks. We evaluate GLAM on vision benchmarks with sequences of up to 50 tasks, significantly extending beyond standard protocols. GLAM achieves the highest accuracy across the evaluated benchmarks. Compared with the baseline attaining the highest average accuracy across benchmarks, it uses 16--19\\% of the trainable parameters and reduces training time by approximately 63--74\\%, demonstrating efficient and scalable continual learning. Our source code is publicly available at https://github.com/atlas-luiss/GLAM."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at the Conference on Lifelong Learning Agents (CoLLAs 2026)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.13211","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:58:29Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:58:30Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-12T02:32:05Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12826","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12826","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12826","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Faustin","familyName":"Adiceam","name":"Adiceam, Faustin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dzmitry","familyName":"Badziahin","name":"Badziahin, Dzmitry","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"On the $P(t)$-adic Littlewood Conjecture in Characteristics $\\ell \\equiv 3\\pmod{4}$"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Number Theory (math.NT)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Combinatorics (math.CO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Dynamical Systems (math.DS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T08:50:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:33:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-18T07:34:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-19T00:25:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-10T18:26:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-08-12T00:05:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Given a prime $p$, the $p$-adic Littlewood Conjecture stands as a well-known arithmetic variant of the celebrated Littlewood Conjecture in Diophantine Approximation. In the same way as the latter, it admits a natural function field analogue depending on the choice of an irreducible polynomial $P(t)$ with coefficients in a field $\\mathbb{K}$. This analogue is referred to as the $P(t)$-adic Littlewood Conjecture ($P(t)$-LC for short).\n $P(t)$-LC is proved to fail for any choice of irreducible polynomial $P(t)$ over any ground field $\\mathbb{K}$ with characteristic $ \\ell \\equiv 3\\pmod{4}$. The counterexample refuting it is shown to present a local arithmetic obstruction emerging from the fact that -1 is not a quadratic residue modulo a prime $\\ell\\equiv 3\\pmod{4}$.\n The theory developed elucidates and generalises all previous approaches towards refuting the conjecture. They were all based on the computer-assisted method initiated by Adiceam, Nesharim and Lunnon (2021) which has been able to establish that $P(t)$-LC fails in some small characteristics (essentially up to 11). This computer-assisted method is, however, unable to provide a general statement as it relies on ad hoc computer verifications which, provided they terminate, refute $P(t)$--LC in a given characteristic. This limitation is overcome by exhibiting an arithmetic obstruction to the validity of $P(t)$--LC in infinitely many characteristics.\n The existence of arithmetic obstructions within the context of $P(t)$--LC leaves the remaining case of odd characteristics $ \\ell\\equiv 1\\pmod{4}$ dependent on their determination. This is shown to hold in an effective and explicit way."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12826","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:49:28Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:49:28Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-12T02:32:04Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12864","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12864","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12864","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jorge Acuña","familyName":"Flores","name":"Flores, Jorge Acuña","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Giuseppe","familyName":"De Nittis","name":"De Nittis, Giuseppe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Javier Lorca","familyName":"Espiro","name":"Espiro, Javier Lorca","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Higher Abelian Quantum Double Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Mathematical Physics (math-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"Primary: 81R15, Secondary:46L30, 47L40, 82B20","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T09:19:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:35:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-11T14:56:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-12T01:01:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This paper develops a rigorous $C^*$-algebraic framework for higher abelian quantum double models, generalizing Kitaev's construction to simplicial complexes of arbitrary finite dimension and local regularity. We fully characterize the frustration-free ground state space through an associated algebra of logical operators: its state space is shown to be homeomorphic to the space of frustration-free ground states, and to obey generalized canonical commutation relations. When the relevant homology and cohomology groups are finite, the logical algebra decomposes into a commutative factor and a full matrix algebra, thereby separating the classical and quantum parts of the frustration-free ground state structure. We further prove that the vanishing of these groups is necessary and sufficient for the core algebra of the model to form a Cartan pair with the full algebra of observables, a property expected to pave the way toward classifying the model's equilibrium (KMS) states, extending recent results for the planar case."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"44 pages. v2: Added a new section (Section 4) on the (C^*)-diagonal structure and corrected minor typos. Keywords:Quantum Double Models, Frustration Free Ground States, Pure States Characterization, $C^*$-algebra, Cartan pair"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12864","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:50:22Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:50:22Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-12T02:32:04Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12393","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12393","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12393","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Reetish","familyName":"Padhi","name":"Padhi, Reetish","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ion Victor","familyName":"Gosea","name":"Gosea, Ion Victor","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Igor Pontes","familyName":"Duff","name":"Duff, Igor Pontes","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Serkan","familyName":"Gugercin","name":"Gugercin, Serkan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Data-driven balanced truncation for linear systems with quadratic outputs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Numerical Analysis (math.NA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Dynamical Systems (math.DS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T19:37:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-12T00:07:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1109/lcsys.2025.3642487","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We develop the framework for a non-intrusive, quadrature-based method for approximate balanced truncation (QuadBT) of linear systems with quadratic outputs, thus extending the applicability of QuadBT, which was originally designed for data-driven balanced truncation of standard linear systems with linear outputs only. The new approach makes use of the time-domain and frequency-domain quadrature-based representation of the system's infinite Gramians, only implicitly. We show that by sampling solely the extended impulse responses of the original system and their derivatives (or the corresponding transfer functions), we construct a reduced-order model that mimics the approximation quality of the intrusive (projection-based) balanced truncation. We validate the proposed framework on a numerical example."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12393","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-09-17T01:39:24Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:39:25Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-12T02:32:03Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.13034","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.13034","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.13034","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cedric","familyName":"Gaberle","name":"Gaberle, Cedric","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Manpreet Singh","familyName":"Jattana","name":"Jattana, Manpreet Singh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Slice-Wise Initial State Optimization to Improve Cost and Accuracy of the VQE on Lattice Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Quantum Physics (quant-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T12:52:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-11T01:29:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1088/1402-4896/ae26f8","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We propose an optimization method for the Variational Quantum Eigensolver (VQE) that combines adaptive and physics-inspired ansatz design. Instead of optimizing multiple layers simultaneously, the ansatz is built incrementally from its operator subsets, enabling subspace optimization that provides better initialization for subsequent steps. This quasi-dynamical approach preserves expressivity and hardware efficiency while avoiding the overhead of operator selection associated with adaptive methods. Benchmarks on one- and two-dimensional Heisenberg and Hubbard models with up to 20 qubits show improved fidelities, reduced function evaluations, or both, compared to fixed-layer VQE. The method is simple, cost-effective, and particularly well-suited for current noisy intermediate-scale quantum (NISQ) devices."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.13034","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-09-17T01:54:17Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:54:18Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-11T04:12:16Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12685","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12685","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12685","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Saumyajit","familyName":"Das","name":"Das, Saumyajit","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tuhin","familyName":"Ghosh","name":"Ghosh, Tuhin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shiqi","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Shiqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Inverse scattering for the fractional Schrödinger equation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Analysis of PDEs (math.AP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"35R30, 35R11, 35P25, 35Q40, 47G30","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T05:19:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:24:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-07T06:05:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-10T00:26:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This article is devoted to studying the inverse scattering for the fractional Schrödinger equation, and in particular we solve the Born approximation problem. Based on the ($p$,$q$)-type resolvent estimate for the fractional Laplacian, we derive an expression for the scattering amplitude of the scattered solution of the fractional Schrödinger equation. We prove the uniqueness of the potential using the scattering amplitude data."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"23 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12685","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:46:07Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:46:08Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-10T02:05:48Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12529","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12529","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12529","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"A.","familyName":"Govindarajan","name":"Govindarajan, A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"M.","familyName":"Mazzei","name":"Mazzei, M.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"H.","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, H.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"L.","familyName":"Tian","name":"Tian, L.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Pulsed Generation of Continuous-Variable Cluster States in a Phononic Quantum Network"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Quantum Physics (quant-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Mesoscale and Nanoscale Physics (cond-mat.mes-hall)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T00:09:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:12:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-06T17:26:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-07T01:01:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1103/qfjb-g83b","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Cluster states are multipartite entangled states that are maximally connected and resilient to decoherence, making them valuable resources for quantum information processing. Continuous-variable (CV) cluster states have been extensively investigated for such applications. Here we present a pulsed protocol for generating CV cluster states in a phononic quantum network composed of phonon waveguides, mechanical resonators, and optical cavities. A key feature of this architecture is its modular design, where pairs of mechanical modes serve as building blocks with only local, tunable interactions between mechanical and cavity modes. We characterize the generated cluster states through the average nullifier of the CV modes. Our numerical results show that high-quality CV cluster states can be generated with moderate squeezing parameters, whereas strong squeezing and the resulting large phonon occupations can degrade the cluster states in the presence of finite dissipation. We further show that, under moderate squeezing and dissipation, the average nullifier can decrease with increasing system size $N$, demonstrating the scalability of the proposed scheme. As a direct application, we demonstrate that distant mechanical modes can be entangled through local measurements."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"14 pages, 7 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12529","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-09-17T01:42:31Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:42:31Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-07T02:14:54Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12862","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12862","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12862","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Noah","familyName":"Kravitz","name":"Kravitz, Noah","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Santiago","familyName":"Morales","name":"Morales, Santiago","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Carl","familyName":"Schildkraut","name":"Schildkraut, Carl","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Statistics of Erdős-Rényi random numerical semigroups"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Combinatorics (math.CO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Commutative Algebra (math.AC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Number Theory (math.NT)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T09:18:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:35:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T14:12:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-06T00:50:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"For $p\u0026gt;0$ a small parameter, let $\\mathcal A \\subseteq \\mathbb{Z}_{\u0026gt;0}$ be a random subset where each positive integer is included independently with probability $p$. We show that, with high probability (as $p \\to 0$), the numerical semigroup $\\langle\\mathcal A\\rangle:=\\{a_1+\\cdots+a_k: k \\geq 0, a_1, \\ldots, a_k \\in \\mathcal A\\}$ generated by $\\mathcal A$ has Frobenius number and genus of size $\\asymp p^{-1}(\\log p^{-1})^2$ and embedding dimension of size $\\asymp (\\log p^{-1})^2$. This resolves an open problem of Bogart and the second author."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"10 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12862","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:50:19Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:50:19Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-06T02:13:46Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.13236","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.13236","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.13236","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fitsum Sileshi","familyName":"Beyene","name":"Beyene, Fitsum Sileshi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Christopher L.","familyName":"Dancy","name":"Dancy, Christopher L.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Layout-Aware OCR for Black Digital Archives with Unsupervised Evaluation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Digital Libraries (cs.DL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T16:43:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-05T01:05:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1109/istas65609.2025.11269615","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Despite their cultural and historical significance, Black digital archives continue to be a structurally underrepresented area in AI research and infrastructure. This is especially evident in efforts to digitize historical Black newspapers, where inconsistent typography, visual degradation, and limited annotated layout data hinder accurate transcription, despite the availability of various systems that claim to handle optical character recognition (OCR) well. In this short paper, we present a layout-aware OCR pipeline tailored for Black newspaper archives and introduce an unsupervised evaluation framework suited to low-resource archival contexts. Our approach integrates synthetic layout generation, model pretraining on augmented data, and a fusion of state-of-the-art You Only Look Once (YOLO) detectors. We used three annotation-free evaluation metrics, the Semantic Coherence Score (SCS), Region Entropy (RE), and Textual Redundancy Score (TRS), which quantify linguistic fluency, informational diversity, and redundancy across OCR regions. Our evaluation on a 400-page dataset from ten Black newspaper titles demonstrates that layout-aware OCR improves structural diversity and reduces redundancy compared to full-page baselines, with modest trade-offs in coherence. Our results highlight the importance of respecting cultural layout logic in AI-driven document understanding and lay the foundation for future community-driven and ethically grounded archival AI systems."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"IEEE-ISTAS conference"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.13236","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-09-17T01:59:04Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:59:04Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-05T02:29:32Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.13086","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.13086","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.13086","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Luca","familyName":"Baracco","name":"Baracco, Luca","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Olga","familyName":"Bernardi","name":"Bernardi, Olga","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anna","familyName":"Florio","name":"Florio, Anna","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alessandra","familyName":"Nardi","name":"Nardi, Alessandra","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Birkhoff attractors for dissipative symplectic billiards"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Dynamical Systems (math.DS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"37C70, 37G35, 37E40","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T13:40:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T02:01:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1007/s10884-026-10528-9","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The aim of the present paper is to propose and study a dissipative variant of symplectic billiards within planar strictly convex domains. The associated billiard map is dissipative, thus it admits a compact invariant set, the so-called Birkhoff attractor. Its complexity depends on the rate of the dissipation as well as on the geometry of the billiard table. We prove that (a) for strong dissipation, the Birkhoff attractor is a normally contracted graph over the zero section; (b) for mild dissipation, the Birkhoff attractor within a centrally symmetric domain is an indecomposable continuum whose restricted dynamics has positive topological entropy. We compare these results with the case of dissipative Birkhoff billiards, studied in a paper by Bernardi-Florio-Leguil"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.13086","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-09-17T01:55:35Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:55:35Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T04:35:33Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12985","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12985","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12985","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alessandro","familyName":"Casini","name":"Casini, Alessandro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Adam","familyName":"McCloskey","name":"McCloskey, Adam","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Luca","familyName":"Rolla","name":"Rolla, Luca","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Raimondo","familyName":"Pala","name":"Pala, Raimondo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Dynamic Local Average Treatment Effects in Time Series"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Econometrics (econ.EM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Economics and business","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T11:47:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:42:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-07-30T19:32:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-03T00:06:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This paper discusses identification, estimation, and inference on dynamic local average treatment effects (LATEs) in instrumental variables (IVs) settings. First, we show that compliers--observations whose treatment status is affected by the instrument--can be identified individually in time series data using smoothness assumptions and local comparisons of treatment assignments. Second, we show that this result enables not only better interpretability of IV estimates but also direct testing of the exclusion restriction by comparing outcomes among identified non-compliers across instrument values. Third, we document pervasive weak identification in applied work using IVs with time series data by surveying recent publications in leading economics journals. However, we find that strong identification often holds in large subsamples for which the instrument induces changes in the treatment. Motivated by this, we introduce a method based on dynamic programming to detect the most strongly-identified subsample and show how to use this subsample to improve estimation and inference. We also develop new identification-robust inference procedures that focus on the most strongly-identified subsample, offering efficiency gains relative to existing full sample identification-robust inference when identification fails over parts of the sample. Finally, we apply our results to heteroskedasticity-based identification of monetary policy effects. We find that about 75% of observations are compliers (i.e., cases where the variance of the policy shifts up on FOMC announcement days), and we fail to reject the exclusion restriction. Estimation using the most strongly-identified subsample helps reconcile conflicting IV and GMM estimates in the literature."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12985","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:53:09Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:53:10Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-03T02:08:04Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12290","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12290","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12290","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jonas C.","familyName":"Ditz","name":"Ditz, Jonas C.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Veronika","familyName":"Lazar","name":"Lazar, Veronika","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Elmar","familyName":"Lichtmeß","name":"Lichtmeß, Elmar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Carola","familyName":"Plesch","name":"Plesch, Carola","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Matthias","familyName":"Heck","name":"Heck, Matthias","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kevin","familyName":"Baum","name":"Baum, Kevin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Markus","familyName":"Langer","name":"Langer, Markus","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Secure human oversight of AI: Threat modeling in a socio-technical context"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computers and Society (cs.CY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T08:22:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:01:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-05T10:08:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-06T01:47:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-30T08:39:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-07-31T00:36:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Human oversight of AI is promoted as a safeguard against risks such as inaccurate outputs, system malfunctions, or violations of fundamental rights, and is mandated in regulation like the European AI Act. Yet debates on human oversight have largely focused on its effectiveness, while overlooking a critical dimension: the security of human oversight. We argue that human oversight creates a new attack surface within the safety, security, and accountability architecture of AI operations. Drawing on cybersecurity perspectives, we model human oversight as an IT application for the purpose of systematic threat modeling of the human oversight process. Threat modeling allows us to identify security risks within human oversight and points towards possible mitigation strategies. Our contributions are: (1) introducing a security perspective on human oversight, (2) offering researchers and practitioners guidance on how to approach their human oversight applications from a security point of view, and (3) providing a systematic overview of attack vectors and hardening strategies to enable secure human oversight of AI."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12290","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:37:03Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:37:04Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-31T02:35:01Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.13283","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.13283","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.13283","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nicholas G.","familyName":"Polson","name":"Polson, Nicholas G.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daniel","familyName":"Zantedeschi","name":"Zantedeschi, Daniel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"De Finetti + Sanov = Bayes: Exchangeable Prediction under Moment Constraints"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Statistics Theory (math.ST)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Methodology (stat.ME)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"60G09, 62F15, 60F10, 94A17","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T17:36:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:58:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-07-16T13:42:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-17T00:47:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-28T23:34:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-07-30T00:12:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We study exchangeable prediction when empirical-moment constraints define each active finite horizon N. The relevant law is the de Finetti m